	myFirst : dictionnary
	aList :
		- aList1
	another : pair

okay so, in chrono order
	Parser(std::string path) :: go find files, make a list of the files
		File(path) :: reads files into a list of string objects
		Tokenizer(std::string filecontent, std::list<Token{string token, uint8 type, u_int level}>)
			self-explanatory :: separates every word/spec character
		TokenTransformer(std::list<Token>)
			handles casts, backslashes, quotes and indentations
		Tree(std::list<Token>)
			reads all tokens and creates a (nb) tree of ymlObjects (dict, list, def)
			recursive mf, reads through tokens and adds to lists, dicts, defs

advice ::
	should delete unnecessary spaces in tokenTransformer
	
possible actual errors ::
	Tokenizer::typeOfValue()
		while (isalnum(*it)) for STRING seems overly restraining - like '.' and '_' --> these are acceptable characters, dude


if i had done it ::
	first i would have kept the files as a map<size_t line num, getline string>
		why consolidate when i need to separate later during the parsing ?
	second i would have done a .clearcomments() that just, deletes all comments
		we don't need or read them, they're only relevant for h.e
		if backslash do indeed do just what i think, same here+line then token concat
	third, something to be said for a .substr(0, 1) -> substr(1) || .substr()==
		using iterators for a 1-sized string seems over-complicated
		and a .rfind("", 0) == 0 feels uncanny
	fourth (oh god i am that guy), the switch case are being under utilized
		code is bulky, often where just one value changes we could cube it
	fifth, while v for loops on simple step-untils
		its just difficult to read at first glance, ill admit, aesthetics
	sixth, space trimming within tokenization (quotes, too ?)
		personal preference, optimization at most
	oh my god there's way too many functionalities
		this is so, so beyond overkill. like. for real.
	
	should i give the tree-ification a try ? it doesn't seem overly complex
	list v dict (indent + '-' v indent ; list of only one kind of value)
	just a simple recursive distrib ::
		(at it) :: checkType(it) -> 0 dict, 1 list, 2 pair, 3 value [or sthg]
			dict :: new dict, read all correct indentation;
				or, read from { to }, commpa-separated;
					then call back on every item before return;
			list :: new list, read all correct indentation of first object type;
				or, read from [ to ], comma separated;
					then call back on every item before return;
			pair :: new pair, read current line and return;
			value :: new value, read current token and return;
	which then gives us a possible tree of
list (name="", std::list<ymlDict>)
	dict (name="server00", std::list<*ymlObject>)
		pair (key="listen", val="7500")
		pair (key="index", val="/html/index.html")
		pair (key="root", val="./server00")
		list (name="locations", std::list<ymlList<ymlObj>>))
			list (name="/scripts", std::list<ymlObj>)
				obj (val="GET")
				obj (val="POST")
			list (name="/html", std::list<ymlObj>)
				obj (val="GET")
			list (name="/uploads", std::list<ymlObj>)
				obj (val="DELETE")
		list (name="error files", std::list<ymlPair>)
			pair (key="404", val="/html/error/404.html")
			pair (key="400", val="/html/error/400.html")
			//etc.
	dict (name="default", std::list<*ymlObject>)
		pair (key="listen", val="8080")
		pair (key="index", val="/html/404.html")
		pair (key="root", val=".")
		//etc.
